<!--
  Quick Settings tile icon, discoverable state: a solid shield with a padlock knocked out of it.

  One path, four windings, evenOdd. Depth alternates fill / hole / fill, which is what lets a single
  flat colour express a cutout:

    1. shield        depth 1 -> filled
    2. padlock       depth 2 -> knocked out
    3. shackle hoop  depth 3 -> filled again, so the arch reads as a hoop and not a solid block
    4. keyhole       depth 3 -> filled again

  Nothing paints its own colour and there is no background: Quick Settings tints the glyph and
  supplies the on/off background, so an icon carrying its own state would contradict the tile it sits
  in. State is shape only — ic_tile_discoverable_off is the outline counterpart and shares this
  shield and lock geometry exactly, so the glyph does not move or resize when the tile is tapped.

  The lock occupies y=6.1..14.7, centring it on y=10.4 against the shield's 3.2..21.2. Deliberately
  well above the shield's geometric midpoint of 12.2: the bottom half tapers to a point and carries
  far less visual weight, so a lock centred arithmetically sits noticeably low. Pushed much higher
  than this and the shackle starts crowding the shield's top edge instead.

  Inside the body (9.7..14.7) the keyhole runs 10.75..13.5, leaving 1.2 below it and 1.05 above. The
  slot used to reach within 0.3 of the body's bottom edge, which read as the keyhole falling out of
  the lock — keep roughly a unit of clearance if this is ever retuned.

  It must also stay inside x=8.1..15.9 and above y≈16.5. Lower or wider and it pushes through the
  shield's taper — invisible here, where both shapes share one fill, but plainly broken in the
  outline state.
-->
